# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in what you need. Everything here is optional:
# with no .env at all the tool searches the keyless engines over a direct connection.
# An exported shell variable always beats this file.
# Read in this order: WEBSEARCH_ENV_FILE if set, then ./.env, then
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/websearch/.env (usually ~/.config/websearch/.env), which is the
# one that applies wherever you run the command. Put it there to set it once.
# Check whatever you set with: websearch doctor

# --- VPN (off by default) ------------------------------------------------------------
# Declares that egress is expected to be tunneled, so `websearch doctor` verifies it
# instead of assuming it. It does not route anything: the tunnel is your VPN app's job.
#   nordvpn  verified against NordVPN's own connection check
#   any      only asserts that some tunnel interface is up
# WEBSEARCH_VPN=nordvpn

# --- Egress proxy --------------------------------------------------------------------
# One proxy for every network path the tool opens, in both processes (the CLI and the
# local SearXNG). Prefer socks5h:// over socks5:// so DNS resolves proxy-side.
# The two NordVPN credentials below are the whole setup: with both filled the proxy is
# on and egress is locked to it, with nothing else to set. WEBSEARCH_PROXY only needs a
# value to pick a different proxy, or to say `off`.
# WEBSEARCH_PROXY=socks5h://user:pass@host:1080
# WEBSEARCH_PROXY=nordvpn
# WEBSEARCH_PROXY=off

# NordVPN service credentials, from the Nord Account dashboard under
# "Set up NordVPN manually". These are NOT your account email and password.
# `websearch proxy setup` scaffolds these lines and prints this file's path;
# `websearch proxy status` says whether they work.
# NORDVPN_USER=
# NORDVPN_PASS=

# Which city the traffic comes out of. `websearch proxy locations` lists them (NordVPN
# runs SOCKS5 exits in the Netherlands, Sweden, and nine US cities); `websearch proxy
# use dallas` writes this line. Unset means the Amsterdam pool.
# NORDVPN_HOST=nl.socks.nordhold.net

# --- Egress lock ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Locked, nothing leaves this machine outside the proxy: a path that has no proxy is
# refused (error code egress_locked) instead of falling back to a direct connection.
# Covers both fetch tiers, the engines, the local SearXNG's own engine requests, the
# installs, and `doctor --baseline`. Loopback and LAN targets are unaffected: they
# never leave. A configured proxy locks on its own; this variable is the explicit
# override in either direction. `websearch proxy lock` writes on, `websearch proxy
# unlock` writes off.
# WEBSEARCH_EGRESS_LOCK=on

# --- Self-hosted SearXNG (off by default) --------------------------------------------
# Start one with: ./docker/searxng/searxng.sh up
# WEBSEARCH_SEARXNG_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8888

# --- Tor (off by default) ------------------------------------------------------------
# On, every request goes through a local Tor SOCKS port, .onion becomes reachable, and
# `websearch web-search --onion` searches the Tor indexes. `websearch tor up` starts one
# and writes this line for you; `websearch tor down` sets it back to off.
# A proxy set above is NOT replaced: it becomes Tor's upstream in torrc, so the path is
# you, then the proxy, then Tor.
# WEBSEARCH_TOR=on

# Point at a Tor you already run instead of letting the tool start one.
# Tor Browser's is on 9150.
# WEBSEARCH_TOR_SOCKS=socks5h://127.0.0.1:9050
# WEBSEARCH_TOR_PORT=9050
